  "slug": "prediction-antimicrobial-peptide-e8c1",
  "summary": "Recent preprints expose benchmark leakage, embedding anisotropy and miscalibrated scores, while generic or consensus models match specialized tools.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is evaluation discipline for sequence and structure predictors. Antimicrobial peptide classifiers lose their near-perfect scores once homology and length are controlled, and length alone separates benchmark classes. Protein language model embedding retrieval is benchmarked against MMseqs2 and Foldseek, with anisotropy degrading remote-homology search and ZCA whitening proposed as a remedy. ProtCOVER applies family-level conformal prediction to give valid coverage for missense variant effect predictors on unseen families. Infernal-GPU recalibrates Rfam E-values while recovering remote structured RNA homologs, and HyPhy 3's WALRUS jointly estimates alignment, phylogeny and selection to cut indel-driven false positives. A parallel theme favors generality over specialization: untuned TabPFN-3.5 matches or beats dedicated genomic prediction methods, and AbPoolRank's predictor-agnostic consensus over mixed structure pools reaches 58.0% Success@1 for antibody–antigen complexes. MicroDUET and FoldMapper extend foundation-model and homology-profile approaches to pathogen surveillance and inverse folding.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
}
